    	For those of you who know about genetics, the end of our chromosomes 
contain a length of protective molecules known as the telomere.  Every time 
the cell divides, the telomere gets shortened.  Eventually, after a certain 
amount of cellular divisions, the telomere is no longer of useful length 
and the cell dies as its DNA becomes corrupted.  This mechanism is thought 
to be the reason organisms age and is also thought to be the reason why 
cloned organisms seem to die abnormally early (such as Dolly the sheep): 
since they are cloned from adult cells, the telomeres are already 
shortened, and thus they live longer.

    	How is this related to Nethack, you ask?  I think gremlins, puddings, 
and everything else that splits should have a counter added to its monster 
struct (in essence, a computerized-version of the telomere).  Thus, the 
monsters would only be able to divide so much.  This would help prevent 
levels getting absolutely overrun by monsters.  Currently, the code 
prevents monsters from splitting if their HP is 1, but in the case of 
puddings at least, they can heal over time, and in a large level, you end 
up fighting a losing battle, as the puddings you aren't in contact with 
heal faster than the ones you can kill that are in reach.

